To ensure that businesses can easily start and carry out service activities, it is important to check and report on authorisation schemes. This reporting applies only to the existence of such schemes, not to the rules or conditions used within them. Chapter III – Freedom of Establishment for Service Providers  
Section 1: Authorisations  
Article 9: Authorisation Schemes 1. Member States must not require businesses to get a licence (authorisation) in order to provide a service, unless the following conditions are met:  
(a) The scheme is necessary to protect public health, consumer safety, animal health, or the urban environment;  
(b) The scheme does not treat businesses from different countries unfairly (no discrimination on nationality basis);  
(c) The scheme is the minimum necessary measure to achieve the public interest objective;  
(d) The scheme does not go beyond what is strictly required (principle of proportionality). Furthermore, this rule does not apply to situations where public authorities decide to set up a public or private company to provide a service, or when public authorities sign contracts for service provision under public procurement rules, since this Directive does not cover public procurement. Also, Member States may revoke authorisations if the conditions for the authorisation are no longer met. However, such revocation must not be used in a way that treats businesses unfairly based on their country of origin.

(c) The country where the service is provided has not taken any actions, or has taken actions that are not enough compared to those mentioned in Article 35(2);  
(d) The measures taken must be reasonable and fit for the purpose. 3. Paragraphs 1 and 2 do not affect any rules already set out in the Community regulations. (b) Necessity: The requirement must be justified by public policy, public security, public health, or environmental protection.  
(c) Proportionality: The requirement must be suitable for achieving its goal and must not go beyond what is strictly necessary. (78) To ensure that services can move freely across the Community and that both service providers and recipients can offer and receive services without borders, it is necessary to clarify what limits a Member State may impose on service providers. It must be clear that the freedom to provide services does not stop a Member State from imposing its own rules – as long as those rules follow the principles in Article 16(1)(a) to (c) – for reasons of public policy, public security, public health, or environmental protection. (79) The Court of Justice has repeatedly ruled that Member States are allowed to take measures to prevent providers from abusing the rules of the internal market. Such abuse must be decided on a case-by-case basis.
